WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.234
-Our next guest is the legendary
and award-winning author

00:00:03.234 --> 00:00:06.067
of more than 50 books,
including "The Blind Assassin,"

00:00:06.067 --> 00:00:08.167
"The Handmaid's Tale,"
and "The Testaments."

00:00:08.167 --> 00:00:11.000
Her latest, "Old Babes
in the Wood," is on sale today.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.834
Please welcome back to the show
the great Margaret Atwood.

00:00:13.834 --> 00:00:15.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.901 --> 00:00:22.701
♪♪

00:00:22.701 --> 00:00:29.534
♪♪

00:00:29.534 --> 00:00:36.334
♪♪

00:00:36.334 --> 00:00:37.801
Welcome back, Margaret.

00:00:37.801 --> 00:00:39.734
-Well, remember
what was happening

00:00:39.734 --> 00:00:41.234
when we last saw each other?

00:00:41.234 --> 00:00:43.033
We were about to have COVID.

00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:45.334
-We were about to have COVID.
It was 2019.

00:00:45.334 --> 00:00:48.133
It was right -- It felt like
the very eve of it.

00:00:48.133 --> 00:00:49.667
-It was, yeah.

00:00:49.667 --> 00:00:52.567
And what's happened
in the interval?

00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:55.467
No time at all has passed.
You look just the same.

00:00:55.467 --> 00:00:56.601
-Thank you very much.

00:00:56.601 --> 00:00:58.367
I think you look
maybe even younger.

00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:03.000
I think you've done
extremely well for yourself.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.968
I do want to talk -- because
the last time you were here,

00:01:04.968 --> 00:01:08.067
we were discussing your
wonderful book "The Testaments,"

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:11.000
and we didn't discuss a thing
that I knew to be true,

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.834
that you were going through
an incredibly tough loss.

00:01:14.834 --> 00:01:17.367
It was a time of grieving
for you.

00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:19.901
And it does seem like this book

00:01:19.901 --> 00:01:23.367
is maybe a means of addressing
the loss that you went through.

00:01:23.367 --> 00:01:27.067
-Yes, well, I walked off the
plane right after Graeme died,

00:01:27.067 --> 00:01:31.033
and I walked right on to
your show in a state of shock.

00:01:31.033 --> 00:01:32.467
-And this was your partner --

00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:34.334
-I can't remember
anything I said to you.

00:01:34.334 --> 00:01:37.167
-Well, it speaks to --
-Except I read your hand.

00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:39.200
-You did.
You gave me a palm reading.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.100
And I remember you said,
"I think COVID's coming,"

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.934
and I'm like, "I don't even know
what that is."

00:01:42.934 --> 00:01:45.801
-I did not say that.
-You did not say that.

00:01:45.801 --> 00:01:47.234
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:47.234 --> 00:01:49.567
-And this was your partner
of over 50 years.

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:53.634
-Yes.
-And did writing this book --

00:01:53.634 --> 00:01:55.968
Was that a catharsis for you?

00:01:55.968 --> 00:01:58.367
-Well, I'd written
these two characters,

00:01:58.367 --> 00:01:59.667
Nell and Tig, before.

00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:02.467
They're in an earlier book
of stories.

00:02:02.467 --> 00:02:06.667
But in the earlier book,
they're both still alive.

00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:09.601
So in some of these stories,
they're both still alive,

00:02:09.601 --> 00:02:13.734
and in some of these stories,
one of them is not.

00:02:13.734 --> 00:02:18.801
So I have to explain to younger
people, at my age --

00:02:18.801 --> 00:02:20.300
which is 32 --

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.100
[ Laughter ]

00:02:24.367 --> 00:02:27.868
At my age, you can expect
people to die.

00:02:27.868 --> 00:02:29.501
It happens.

00:02:29.501 --> 00:02:32.567
And it's different
from a younger person dying,

00:02:32.567 --> 00:02:35.000
and it's really different
from a child dying.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.567
So those are tragedies.

00:02:37.567 --> 00:02:44.334
And this, after the age of 80
or so, is the circle of life.

00:02:44.334 --> 00:02:47.801
-Does that make it easier
to process, though?

00:02:47.801 --> 00:02:48.901
-No, it doesn't.

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:50.267
-I would imagine it can't be,
right?

00:02:50.267 --> 00:02:53.501
-It doesn't,
but on the other hand,

00:02:53.501 --> 00:02:56.968
Graeme was a very wise person.

00:02:56.968 --> 00:03:01.467
He had had his diagnosis
in 2012.

00:03:01.467 --> 00:03:05.167
He had a particular kind
of vascular dementia

00:03:05.167 --> 00:03:08.901
that causes you to die
of a cerebral hemorrhage,

00:03:08.901 --> 00:03:10.701
which is what he did.

00:03:10.701 --> 00:03:15.067
His family had a habit of dying
in London, England,

00:03:15.067 --> 00:03:17.834
and so did he.
-Alright, well...

00:03:17.834 --> 00:03:20.934
You wouldn't think that would
be genetic, but there you go.

00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:22.367
-It's not genetic.

00:03:22.367 --> 00:03:26.100
I think it's sort of, you know,
quasi-intentional.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.934
-There you go.
-I don't know.

00:03:28.934 --> 00:03:32.100
-Very fitting because
this book is very beautiful

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.434
and it does touch on grief and
loss, but it's also very funny.

00:03:35.434 --> 00:03:39.567
There's an opening story
about this couple

00:03:39.567 --> 00:03:42.834
going to a first-aid class,
and --

00:03:42.834 --> 00:03:44.534
-It's all true.
-Is it all true?

00:03:44.534 --> 00:03:45.834
So you went
to a first-aid class.

00:03:45.834 --> 00:03:47.667
-We absolutely had to go to one,

00:03:47.667 --> 00:03:51.334
and everybody there was
considerably younger than us.

00:03:51.334 --> 00:03:53.400
So our thing was, you know what?

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:57.100
You find the person unconscious
on the sidewalk.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:00.234
One person calls 911.

00:04:00.234 --> 00:04:02.300
The other one
does the mouth-to-mouth,

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.167
and then the first one

00:04:04.167 --> 00:04:08.367
has to help the second one
up off the floor.

00:04:08.367 --> 00:04:12.133
But, you know, if there's
a red plastic torso

00:04:12.133 --> 00:04:16.400
with a tipped-back head in
trouble, I'll be there for them.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:18.968
[ Laughter ]
-Plastic, ideally plastic.

00:04:18.968 --> 00:04:21.467
Yeah.

00:04:21.467 --> 00:04:24.467
You know, "Handmaid's Tale"
has been coming up again.

00:04:24.467 --> 00:04:27.901
Obviously, you know, it's a book
that's back in the public eye.

00:04:27.901 --> 00:04:30.501
But it also is a book that's
showing up on banned book lists.

00:04:30.501 --> 00:04:33.567
I mean,
this is something that --

00:04:33.567 --> 00:04:35.767
another thing
that you can't believe

00:04:35.767 --> 00:04:37.467
one's guard can be put down on

00:04:37.467 --> 00:04:39.734
that they would ever ban books
again, but of course they are.

00:04:39.734 --> 00:04:41.033
You took it in your own hands

00:04:41.033 --> 00:04:43.367
when you heard that there was
maybe some book banning.

00:04:43.367 --> 00:04:45.834
Explain to us exactly
what you're doing here.

00:04:45.834 --> 00:04:47.934
[ Laughter,
cheers and applause ]

00:04:51.934 --> 00:04:53.300
-Okay.

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:56.100
This was
the unburnable book project.

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.234
-Okay.
-Yeah.

00:04:57.234 --> 00:04:59.701
So that is actually
an unburnable book,

00:04:59.701 --> 00:05:02.968
and we did a little video of it.

00:05:02.968 --> 00:05:05.467
It actually took them
about five tries

00:05:05.467 --> 00:05:09.968
to make one that wouldn't burn,
but they did manage it.

00:05:09.968 --> 00:05:12.534
And that is
a real flamethrower.

00:05:12.534 --> 00:05:16.167
-Did you take a class for that,
Margaret?

00:05:16.167 --> 00:05:17.267
-No.

00:05:17.267 --> 00:05:18.400
[ Laughter ]

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.501
-You seem very confident
with it, I will say.

00:05:20.501 --> 00:05:21.901
-Yes.
They were a little worried.

00:05:21.901 --> 00:05:23.701
And after we had done the shoot,

00:05:23.701 --> 00:05:27.801
they did take it away from me
quite quickly.

00:05:27.801 --> 00:05:29.667
-Well, it certainly
would be very ironic

00:05:29.667 --> 00:05:31.834
if it's like, "Good news,
the book didn't burn.

00:05:31.834 --> 00:05:34.100
Bad news...

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.634
we lost Margaret."

00:05:35.634 --> 00:05:37.701
-No, it wouldn't have been me
that they lost.

00:05:37.701 --> 00:05:40.100
It would have been --
-That's true.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:43.567
You did know well enough
to point it out.

00:05:43.567 --> 00:05:45.868
-None of my enemies
were in the room.

00:05:45.868 --> 00:05:47.567
-Well, that's alright.

00:05:47.567 --> 00:05:51.434
Just for insurance reasons,
they couldn't take that risk.

00:05:51.434 --> 00:05:53.534
You know, obviously,
over 50 books.

00:05:53.534 --> 00:05:54.634
You're incredibly well-known,

00:05:54.634 --> 00:05:57.567
but I do enjoy hearing stories
like this

00:05:57.567 --> 00:05:59.634
because, you know,
it reminds us all to be humble.

00:05:59.634 --> 00:06:02.100
Is it true that you had
a book signing once

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:03.934
where no one showed up?

00:06:03.934 --> 00:06:06.133
-Oh, it was so humiliating.

00:06:06.133 --> 00:06:12.467
Yeah, so it was in a mall in
Winnipeg on a Tuesday afternoon.

00:06:12.467 --> 00:06:13.367
-Okay.

00:06:13.367 --> 00:06:15.067
-Nobody was in the mall,

00:06:15.067 --> 00:06:17.934
and certainly nobody
was in the bookstore.

00:06:17.934 --> 00:06:21.868
There I sat with my little book,
my little table,

00:06:21.868 --> 00:06:25.934
and outside in the mall hallway,
as it were,

00:06:25.934 --> 00:06:29.434
I saw this guy coming -- plunk,
plunk, plunk, plunk, plunk.

00:06:29.434 --> 00:06:32.200
And I thought,
"Here comes my customer."

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:33.901
He opens the door.
He comes up to me.

00:06:33.901 --> 00:06:35.934
Plunk, plunk,
plunk, plunk, plunk.

00:06:35.934 --> 00:06:38.634
And he says,
"Where's the Scotch tape?"

00:06:38.634 --> 00:06:40.300
[ Laughter ]

00:06:42.067 --> 00:06:44.334
And I say...

00:06:44.334 --> 00:06:45.968
"I think it's at the back."

00:06:45.968 --> 00:06:48.334
He says, "Thanks."
That was it.

00:06:48.334 --> 00:06:50.234
-That was it?
That was the entirety of it?

00:06:50.234 --> 00:06:51.601
-That was it.

00:06:51.601 --> 00:06:53.000
-Well, I mean, they say
there's a rule in publishing.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:55.834
Never do a book signing in
a Winnipeg mall on a Tuesday.

00:06:55.834 --> 00:06:57.767
[ Laughter ]

00:06:57.767 --> 00:07:01.501
-Well, my first book signing
was even better -- or worse --

00:07:01.501 --> 00:07:04.968
because it was in the men's
sock and underwear department

00:07:04.968 --> 00:07:08.601
of the Hudson's Bay Company,
a department store,

00:07:08.601 --> 00:07:13.000
in Edmonton, Alberta, in 1969.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:15.033
Why was it there?
I don't know.

00:07:15.033 --> 00:07:18.734
It was near an escalator.

00:07:18.734 --> 00:07:23.100
I think the publicist, whose
first week on the job it was,

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:26.434
thought, "Escalator,
people will go up and down.

00:07:26.434 --> 00:07:30.400
They'll see Margaret sitting
amongst the jockey shorts

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:34.734
with her book called
'The Edible Woman'

00:07:34.734 --> 00:07:38.267
and they will think, 'I would
like to buy one of those.'"

00:07:38.267 --> 00:07:39.534
That didn't happen.

00:07:39.534 --> 00:07:40.934
-Then you have guys
who'd come home

00:07:40.934 --> 00:07:42.234
and they'd be like,
"Did you get socks?"

00:07:42.234 --> 00:07:45.467
It's like, "I totally forgot.
I got this signed book."

00:07:45.467 --> 00:07:48.200
-What really happened
was that I --

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.167
It's a lot of clomping
in these stories.

00:07:50.167 --> 00:07:52.567
These ones had galoshes on,
okay?

00:07:52.567 --> 00:07:55.033
So galoshes, galoshes, galoshes.

00:07:55.033 --> 00:07:56.734
Uhh.

00:07:56.734 --> 00:07:58.234
They ran away very fast.

00:07:58.234 --> 00:08:00.634
I could hear their galoshes.

00:08:00.634 --> 00:08:03.667
-Well, now I believe you hear
everyone running to you,

00:08:03.667 --> 00:08:04.901
running to your work.

00:08:04.901 --> 00:08:06.367
And thank you so much
for being here.

00:08:06.367 --> 00:08:07.834
It's such a delight
to have you back.

00:08:07.834 --> 00:08:10.167
That's Margaret Atwood,
everybody.

00:08:10.167 --> 00:08:11.501
"Old Babes in the Wood"

00:08:11.501 --> 00:08:14.033
is available now
wherever you buy your books.

00:08:14.033 --> 00:08:16.667
But please support local
and independent bookstores

00:08:16.667 --> 00:08:18.067
and malls in Winnipeg.

00:08:18.067 --> 00:08:19.133
We'll be right back.

